
1) what is Spring Cloud Config Server?
Spring Cloud Config Server is a central place to manage external configuration for applications across all environments in a
distributed system. It is part of the Spring Cloud ecosystem and works well with Spring Boot applications,
enabling you to centralize and version-control your application configuration (typically using Git,
but also supports other sources like SVN or local files).

🔧 Key Features
Centralized Configuration Management
All your microservices fetch their configuration from a single source (e.g., Git repo) via the Config Server.

Environment-Specific Configuration
Supports profiles like application-dev.yml, application-prod.yml, etc.

Version Control Integration
Works with Git, allowing you to track configuration changes over time.

Dynamic Updates (with Spring Cloud Bus)
With Spring Cloud Bus + message broker (like RabbitMQ/Kafka), you can broadcast config changes without restarting services.

🏗️ Architecture

           [ Git / SVN / Local FS ]
                    ↑
                    |
         Spring Cloud Config Server
                    ↑
                    |
     ---------------------------------
     |         |           |         |
  Service A  Service B  Service C  Service D


🛠️ How It Works
*You set up a Spring Boot application and annotate it with @EnableConfigServer.
*Configure the location of the config repository (e.g., Git URL) in application.yml of the config server.
*Your client services include the dependency spring-cloud-starter-config and specify the spring.config.import=optional:configserver: property.
*At startup, the client service fetches its configuration from the config server.

